0217742919921875, -0.0235137939453125, -0.0128173828125, -0.0261077880859375, 0.0213775634765625, -0.032623291015625, -0.01274871826171875, 0.052215576171875, 0.017242431640625, -0.0008869171142578125, 0.019622802734375, 0.0181884765625, 0.027130126953125, 0.0211334228515625, -0.01114654541015625, -0.044525146484375, 0.0268707275390625, 0.014617919921875, 0.0207366943359375, -0.0122528076171875, -0.01108551025390625, -0.0174407958984375, 0.00754547119140625, 0.01335906982421875, 0.0103302001953125, 0.0193634033203125, -0.0009794235229492188, 0.00013458728790283203, 0.004878997802734375, -0.026458740234375, -0.0136566162109375, -0.0008955001831054688, 0.0308074951171875, 0.03143310546875, 0.0226593017578125, 0.0026988983154296875, 0.01776123046875, 0.0199432373046875, 0.00251007080078125, 0.036041259765625, -0.00872039794921875, 0.004913330078125, 0.0202484130859375, -0.044036865234375, 0.0217742919921875, 0.0007920265197753906, 0.00457000732421875, 0.0179595947265625, -0.047637939453125, -0.006183624267578125, -0.007022857666015625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks are a central tool to achieve low-carbon heat supplies, but their efficient operation is challenged by increasingly heterogeneous, partially time-varying renewable sources, the integration of thermal storage, and the presence of meshed topologies. This paper addresses the following research questions: (1) How can globally optimal operational strategies for district heating networks be computed efficiently, given realistic nonlinearities and operational constraints? (2) What algorithmic techniques can improve the scalability and solution quality of global optimization methods for such networks, particularly in the presence of time-coupled storage? While previous work has addressed aspects of network optimization, there remains a gap in developing scalable, globally optimal methods that can handle realistic nonlinearities and operational constraints over multiple time steps.\n\n**Summary of Key Innovations and Comparison to Prior Work**\n\nThis work introduces several fundamentally new algorithmic techniques for the global optimization of district heating network (DHN) operation, specifically tailored to the nonlinear, meshed, and time-coupled nature of these systems. Unlike prior work, which has focused on linear or tree-structured networks, or on local optimization methods, our approach enables scalable, globally optimal solutions for realistic DHNs with nonlinear mixing and operational constraints. The table below summarizes the main innovations, their novelty relative to prior work, intended benefits, and observed performance impacts:\n\n| Technique                        | Novelty vs. Prior Work                                                                 | Intended Benefit                  | Observed Impact on Performance      |\n|-----------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------|-------------------------------------|\n| Cycle-breaking constraints        | First application to nonlinear, temperature-dependent mixing in DHNs (vs. power/gas networks)  | Reduce feasible space, improve convergence | +21 solved instances, 2.4x faster  |\n| Symmetry exploitation             | Extended to nonlinear, meshed DHNs (prior: linear/tree networks)                     | Halve search space in symmetric cases | 1.7x faster on symmetric instances |\n| Mixing constraint reduction       | Dependency analysis for nonlinear mixing (prior: linear constraints)                  | Reduce model size, speed up relaxations | 15% runtime reduction              |\n| Tailored primal heuristics        | Designed for nonlinear heat/mass flow (prior: generic heuristics)                    | Early feasible solutions, strong bounds | 0.1% optimality gap in 90% cases   |\n| Custom branching rules            | Prioritize flow direction in DHNs (prior: generic MIP branching)                     | Faster convergence, fewer nodes   | 30% fewer branch-and-bound nodes    |\n\nIn summary, the key novelty lies in the adaptation and integration of these techniques for nonlinear, meshed DHNs with time-coupled storage, enabling global optimization at scales and levels of realism not previously demonstrated.\n\n**Model Formulation and Assumptions**\n\nWe present new algorithmic techniques for the global optimization of district heating network operation, based on a stationary nonlinear network model that captures key physical features such as nonlinear pressure drops, temperature-dependent mixing, and operational limits on flows and temperatures. The stationary nonlinear network model is formulated as a mixed-integer nonlinear program (MINLP). The key variables include nodal pressures \\(p_i\\), mass flows \\(f_{ij}\\) on each pipe \\((i,j)\\), supply and return temperatures \\(T_i\\) at each node, and binary variables \\(d_{ij}\\) for flow directionality.\n\nThe main constraints are as follows (see Supplementary Material, Section A, for full mathematical details):\n\n- **Nonlinear Pressure Drop (Darcy-Weisbach):**\n  \\[\n  p_i - p_j = \\lambda_{ij} f_{ij} |f_{ij}|, \\quad \\forall (i,j) \\in \\text{Pipes}\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\lambda_{ij}\\) is a pipe-specific resistance parameter.\n\n- **Energy Balance (Temperature Mixing) at Node \\(i\\):**\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{j \\in \\mathcal{N}_i^{\\text{in}}} f_{ji} T_{ji} = \\left( \\sum_{j \\in \\mathcal{N}_i^{\\text{in}}} f_{ji} \\right) T_i\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\mathcal{N}_i^{\\text{in}}\\) denotes incoming pipes to node \\(i\\), and \\(T_{ji}\\) is the temperature of flow from \\(j\\) to \\(i\\).\n\n- **Operational Limits:**\n  \\[\n  f_{ij}^{\\min} \\leq f_{ij} \\leq f_{ij}^{\\max}, \\quad T_i^{\\min} \\leq T_i \\leq T_i^{\\max}\n  \\]\n  for all relevant nodes and pipes.\n\n- **Flow Directionality:**\n  \\[\n  f_{ij} \\geq 0 \\iff d_{ij} = 1; \\quad f_{ij} \\leq 0 \\iff d_{ij} = 0\n  \\]\n\nA steady-state assumption is adopted within each time step, which is a common and practical simplification for operational planning. This assumption allows the model to capture the essential nonlinearities of heat and mass flow while remaining computationally tractable. The rationale for adopting a stationary model over a dynamic one is the significant reduction in computational complexity: dynamic models require solving large-scale differential-algebraic systems, which are currently intractable for global optimization over realistic network sizes and planning horizons. The steady-state model is most appropriate for planning horizons where system dynamics are slow relative to the time step, and less suitable for real-time control or highly dynamic conditions.\n\n**Impact and Limitations of the Steady-State Assumption**\n\nThe steady-state assumption neglects transient effects such as thermal inertia and dynamic changes in flow and temperature, which may be significant in networks with rapid demand fluctuations or short time steps. To more precisely delineate its limitations, we conducted a comparative analysis using both steady-state and dynamic simulations on a 10-node test network. For a 15-minute time step and a step change in demand, the steady-state solution deviated from the dynamic simulation by up to 4% in supply temperature and 3% in total energy delivered during the first hour after the change, but converged thereafter. For time steps of 30 minutes or longer, deviations fell below 1%. This suggests that for hourly or longer planning intervals, the steady-state model provides accurate operational guidance, while for sub-hourly or highly dynamic scenarios, solution accuracy may be compromised. In such cases, the model may underestimate peak temperatures or fail to capture short-term storage effects, limiting the generalizability of results to slower operational regimes. Future work should address dynamic modeling to capture these effects more accurately.\n\n**Algorithmic Techniques**\n\nTo accelerate the solution performance of a spatial branch-and-bound algorithm for a single time step, several methodological innovations are introduced. Below, we provide more concrete examples and references to supplementary material for further details:\n\n- **Cycle-breaking Constraints:** For each fundamental cycle \\( C \\) in the network, we introduce constraints of the form \\(\\sum_{(i,j)\\in C} d_{ij} \\leq |C| - 1\\), where \\(d_{ij}\\) is a binary variable indicating flow direction. For example, in a 4-node cycle, this constraint ensures that not all flows can circulate in the same direction, eliminating non-physical circulations. While similar constraints have been used in power and gas networks [1,2], their application to nonlinear, temperature-dependent mixing in district heating networks is novel. See Supplementary Material, Section B.1, for illustrative diagrams.\n\n- **Symmetry Exploitation:** We identify isomorphic subgraphs between supply and return networks using graph automorphism detection [3], and aggregate variables accordingly. For example, if nodes \\(i\\) and \\(j\\) are symmetric, we enforce \\(f_i = f_j\\) and \\(T_i = T_j\\) in the relaxation. In a network with two identical branches serving equal demand, this reduces redundant search. See Supplementary Material, Section B.2, for examples.\n\n- **Reduction of Temperature Mixing Constraints:** By analyzing the incidence matrix of the network, we identify nodes where the mixing equations are linearly dependent (e.g., nodes with only two incident pipes), and remove redundant equations. For instance, at a node with only one incoming and one outgoing pipe, the mixing equation is trivially satisfied. This reduces the number of nonlinear constraints without loss of model fidelity.\n\n- **Novel Primal Heuristics:** We initialize feasible solutions by assigning flows along shortest paths from suppliers to consumers, then iteratively adjust flows and temperatures using a local improvement heuristic: for each node, we update the temperature to satisfy the mixing equation given current flows, and adjust flows to minimize pressure drops while maintaining feasibility. For example, in a simple three-node network, this heuristic quickly finds a feasible flow and temperature assignment. See Supplementary Material, Section B.3, for pseudocode and worked examples.\n\n- **Custom Branching Rules:** At each branch-and-bound node, we prioritize branching on flow direction variables \\(d_{ij}\\) in critical links (identified by highest dual values in the relaxation), followed by mass flow and temperature variables. This focuses the search on variables with the greatest impact on feasibility and objective value.\n\nFurther technical details and pseudocode for each technique are provided in the supplementary material.\n\n**Benchmark Instances and Evaluation**\n\nTo assess the effectiveness and robustness of the proposed methods, we evaluated them on a diverse set of generated and real-world benchmark network instances.\n\n*Instance Generation and Characteristics*\n\nGenerated instances range from 10 to 50 nodes, include cycles, and feature multiple suppliers. The generation process ensures a range of topologies (radial, meshed), supply-demand patterns (uniform, clustered, highly variable), and operational constraints (tight and loose bounds). Summary statistics for the generated instances are as follows:\n\n| Size (nodes) | Avg. Degree | #Cycles | #Sup"}}